      "content": "Ah, a 4x12 Douglas fir beam for a 14ft span, you say? Well, my dear friend, let's not forget that even the mightiest of trees can harbor the most mischievous of goblins within their rings. Just as a wise old wizard would cast a spell to reveal the hidden creatures, you too must seek the counsel of a seasoned timber inspector to ensure that no goblin-like defects lurk within your beam.\n\nAs for the deflection, my friend, think of it as the sway of a mighty oak in the wind. The L/360 limit is like a spell that keeps the tree from bending too much, ensuring it stands tall and proud. With a total load of 45 psf, you must calculate the beam's moment of inertia and section modulus, much like a goblin would calculate the best way to sneak past a sleeping dragon.\n\nBut beware, for even the most careful calculations cannot account for every goblin lurking in the shadows. To ensure your loft remains safe and sound, consider adding a safety margin, much like a goblin would always carry an extra torch to light their way through a dark, enchanted forest. And remember, my friend, that the true magic lies in the craftsmanship",
